    "summary": "High‑octane first set, epic Meat sequence in second set",
    "blurb": "The show shines on the second‑set Meat suite – Truckin' → Drums → The Other One → El Paso → Wharf Rat – with Jerry delivering a blistering solo on Truckin'. The first set is solid but less remarkable. Audio from audience sources is poor, making this a listen‑only for completists.",
    "review": "April 7, 1972 at Wembley Empire Pool captured the Grateful Dead in full, rolling form. While the first set runs through classics like \"Greatest Story Ever Told\" and \"Sugaree\" with competent energy, it’s the second‑set that cements this night as a tour highlight. The legendary Meat sequence erupts: Jerry’s soaring lead on \"Truckin'\", Bill Kreutzmann’s otherworldly drumming under the drums break, and the band rides deep into a sprawling \"The Other One\" before slipping into an exuberant \"El Paso\" and landing on a heartfelt \"Wharf Rat\". Keith Godchaux weaves deftly with Jerry during the jam, and Phil Lesh drops “bass bombs” that thicken the low end. Unfortunately both available audience recordings are riddled with muffled, crackly sound – one described as sounding like it was recorded from the bottom of a pool – so the performance is best appreciated for its historical significance rather than sonic fidelity.\n\nFor Deadheads who crave completeness, this show offers rare moments that define the Europe ’72 tour, even if you’ll need to brace for sub‑par audio. If you can overlook the hiss and drop‑outs, the second set delivers a quintessential glimpse of the band at a creative peak.",
